/* Generated by build/build-data.mjs from sources/word-families.json — do not edit by hand. */
window.POP_FAMILIES = [{"id":"power","name":"Power, Strength & Authority","lang":"Greek","english":["power","strength","might","authority","working","dominion","ruler"],"intro":"English Bibles say ‘power’ for at least six different Greek words. Paul stacks four of them in one breath in Ephesians 1:19 and lists four ranks of spiritual power in Ephesians 1:21. In Colossians 1:13 the WEB's ‘power of darkness’ is not δύναμις at all but ἐξουσία — authority. Knowing which word stands behind ‘power’ tells you what kind of power is meant.","members":[{"strongs":"G1411","sense":"Inherent ability — the capacity to act and to accomplish. Also used for miracles (‘mighty works’), because a miracle is power on display.","anchor":"Acts 1:8","en":"power","text":"But you will receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you. You will be witnesses to me in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the uttermost parts of the earth.”"},{"strongs":"G1743","sense":"To empower, to strengthen inwardly — to put δύναμις into someone; Christ enabling His servants.","anchor":"Philippians 4:13","en":"strengthens","text":"I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me."},{"strongs":"G1753","sense":"Power in operation — energy actually being exerted. Used in the New Testament almost only of God's (or Satan's) power at work.","anchor":"Ephesians 3:7","en":"working","text":"of which I was made a servant, according to the gift of that grace of God which was given me according to the working of his power."},{"strongs":"G1754","sense":"To be at work, to operate effectively — the verb of ἐνέργεια: God's power actively working in His people and His word.","anchor":"Philippians 2:13","en":"works","text":"For it is God who works in you both to will and to work, for his good pleasure."},{"strongs":"G2904","sense":"Dominion-strength — power that prevails and rules. Often in doxologies: to Him be the power forever.","anchor":"Hebrews 2:14","en":"power","text":"Since then the children have shared in flesh and blood, he also himself in the same way partook of the same, that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil,"},{"strongs":"G2479","sense":"Strength, force, might — the raw capacity a person possesses, as in loving God with all your strength.","anchor":"Mark 12:30","en":"strength","text":"you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your mind, and with all your strength.’ This is the first commandment."},{"strongs":"G1849","sense":"Authority — the right to act, delegated or given. Not how strong you are, but what you are authorised to do.","anchor":"Matthew 28:18","en":"authority","text":"Jesus came to them and spoke to them, saying, “All authority has been given to me in heaven and on earth."},{"strongs":"G746","sense":"Rule, principality — a position of first rank (also means ‘beginning’). Used for ruling powers, human and spiritual.","anchor":"Ephesians 1:21","en":"rule","text":"far above all rule, and authority, and power, and dominion, and every name that is named, not only in this age, but also in that which is to come."},{"strongs":"G1413","sense":"Ruler, sovereign — one who holds δύναμις; God as the only absolute Sovereign.","anchor":"1 Timothy 6:15","en":"Ruler","text":"which in its own times he will show, who is the blessed and only Ruler, the King of kings, and Lord of lords;"},{"strongs":"G2003","sense":"The authority of a command — an order given with the right to give it. In Titus 2:15 the WEB renders it ‘authority’, but it is not ἐξουσία: it is the weight of God’s commandment behind the teacher (the same word is ‘commandment’ in Titus 1:3).","anchor":"Titus 2:15","en":"authority","text":"Say these things and exhort and reprove with all authority. Let no man despise you."},{"strongs":"G1756","sense":"Active, effective, at work — the adjective of ἐνέργεια: God’s word is not inert but operating.","anchor":"Hebrews 4:12","en":"active","text":"For the word of God is living and active, and sharper than any two-edged sword, piercing even to the dividing of soul and spirit, of both joints and marrow, and is able to discern the thoughts and intentions of the heart."},{"strongs":"G1412","sense":"To be made strong, empowered — from δύναμις: the heroes of faith ‘from weakness were made strong’ (Hebrews 11:34).","anchor":"Hebrews 11:34","en":"strong","text":"quenched the power of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, from weakness were made strong, grew mighty in war, and caused foreign armies to flee."},{"strongs":"G2478","sense":"Strong, mighty — the adjective of ἰσχύς: physical or moral strength (Hebrews 11:34 ‘mighty in war’).","anchor":"Hebrews 11:34","en":"mighty","text":"quenched the power of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, from weakness were made strong, grew mighty in war, and caused foreign armies to flee."},{"strongs":"G1410","sense":"To be able, to have power to — the everyday verb of the δύναμις family.","anchor":"Hebrews 7:25","en":"able","text":"Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through him, seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them."}]},{"id":"signs","name":"Signs, Wonders & Mighty Works","lang":"Greek","english":["signs","wonders","mighty works","power"],"intro":"The New Testament describes miracles with three words, often together: δύναμις (a mighty work — the power behind it), σημεῖον (a sign — what it points to) and τέρας (a wonder — the amazement it causes). God attested Jesus with all three (Acts 2:22). 2 Thessalonians 2:9 warns that the lawless one will counterfeit all three: ‘power and signs and lying wonders’.","members":[{"strongs":"G1411","sense":"Mighty work — a miracle seen as an act of power.","anchor":"2 Corinthians 12:12","en":"mighty works","text":"Truly the signs of an apostle were worked among you in all perseverance, in signs and wonders and mighty works."},{"strongs":"G4592","sense":"Sign — a miracle seen as pointing beyond itself, revealing who is at work (John's word for Jesus' miracles).","anchor":"John 2:11","en":"signs","text":"This beginning of his signs Jesus did in Cana of Galilee, and revealed his glory; and his disciples believed in him."},{"strongs":"G5059","sense":"Wonder, portent — a miracle seen as something astonishing; never used alone of Jesus' miracles in the New Testament.","anchor":"Acts 2:22","en":"wonders","text":"“Men of Israel, hear these words! Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved by God to you by mighty works and wonders and signs which God did by him among you, even as you yourselves know,"}]},{"id":"time","name":"Time, Season & Age","lang":"Greek","english":["time","season","hour","age","day"],"intro":"Greek separates kinds of time that English blurs together. Acts 1:7 sets ‘times’ (χρόνος) beside ‘seasons’ (καιρός): length of time and the right moment. In John 17:1 the WEB's ‘the time has come’ is ὥρα, the appointed hour. Two adverbs built on καιρός, εὐκαίρως and ἀκαίρως, set the favourable moment against the unfavourable one in 2 Timothy 4:2. Hebrews adds σήμερον, ‘today’: the present day of opportunity.","members":[{"strongs":"G5550","sense":"Chronological time — duration, a span of time, time as it passes (the root of ‘chronology’).","anchor":"Galatians 4:4","en":"time","text":"But when the fullness of the time came, God sent out his Son, born to a woman, born under the law,"},{"strongs":"G2540","sense":"The right moment — a season, an opportunity, a decisive time that must be seized.","anchor":"Mark 1:15","en":"time","text":"and saying, “The time is fulfilled, and God’s Kingdom is at hand! Repent, and believe in the Good News.”"},{"strongs":"G2122","sense":"‘Well-timed’ — εὐ (good) + καιρός: at a convenient, opportune moment, when the season is favourable.","anchor":"2 Timothy 4:2","en":"in season","text":"preach the word; be urgent in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with all patience and teaching."},{"strongs":"G171","sense":"‘Ill-timed’ — ἀ (not) + καιρός: at an inconvenient, unfavourable moment. Paired with εὐκαίρως, it means ‘whether the moment suits or not’.","anchor":"2 Timothy 4:2","en":"out of season","text":"preach the word; be urgent in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with all patience and teaching."},{"strongs":"G5610","sense":"Hour — literally an hour, and in John the appointed hour of Jesus' death and glory.","anchor":"John 17:1","en":"time","text":"Jesus said these things, and lifting up his eyes to heaven, he said, “Father, the time has come. Glorify your Son, that your Son may also glorify you;"},{"strongs":"G165","sense":"Age, era — a long period with its own character (‘this present evil age’), and in the plural, eternity.","anchor":"Galatians 1:4","en":"age","text":"who gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us out of this present evil age, according to the will of our God and Father—"},{"strongs":"G2250","sense":"Day — a literal day, and ‘the day’ of the Lord's return and judgment.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 5:2","en":"day","text":"For you yourselves know well that the day of the Lord comes like a thief in the night."},{"strongs":"G2121","sense":"Well-timed — εὖ + καιρός: help that arrives at exactly the right moment.","anchor":"Hebrews 4:16","en":"time of need","text":"Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need."},{"strongs":"G4340","sense":"For a season, temporary — πρός + καιρός: lasting only for a time, like the ‘pleasures of sin for a time’ Moses refused (Hebrews 11:25).","anchor":"Hebrews 11:25","en":"time","text":"choosing rather to share ill treatment with God’s people, than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a time;"},{"strongs":"G4594","sense":"Today — this very day. In Hebrews it is the day of opportunity to hear God’s voice (3:7–4:11), and the ‘today’ of Christ who is the same yesterday, today and forever (13:8).","anchor":"Hebrews 4:7","en":"today","text":"he again defines a certain day, today, saying through David so long a time afterward (just as has been said), “Today if you will hear his voice, don’t harden your hearts.”"},{"strongs":"G5549","sense":"To take time, delay — from χρόνος: ‘he will not wait’ (Hebrews 10:37).","anchor":"Hebrews 10:37","en":"wait","text":"“In a very little while, he who comes will come, and will not wait."}]},{"id":"love","name":"Love & Affection","lang":"Greek","english":["love","affection","affectionate","lovers"],"intro":"In John 21:15 Jesus asks with ἀγαπάω and Peter answers with φιλέω; the WEB marks the difference as ‘love’ and ‘affection’. The common Greek word ἔρως (romantic desire) never appears in the New Testament. 2 Timothy 3:2–4 adds a row of φιλ- compounds — lovers of self, of money, of pleasure, rather than lovers of God: love itself misdirected. Titus adds the bright side of the φιλ- compounds: lover of good, of guests, of husband and children — and God’s own φιλανθρωπία, His love toward mankind.","members":[{"strongs":"G25","sense":"To love by choice and commitment — self-giving love that seeks the other's good, the love God shows.","anchor":"John 3:16","en":"loved","text":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life."},{"strongs":"G26","sense":"Love (noun) — the self-giving love described in 1 Corinthians 13 and poured out by God.","anchor":"1 Corinthians 13:4","en":"love","text":"Love is patient and is kind; love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud,"},{"strongs":"G5368","sense":"To be fond of, have warm affection for — the love of friends (φίλος, friend).","anchor":"John 21:15","en":"affection","text":"So when they had eaten their breakfast, Jesus said to Simon Peter, “Simon, son of Jonah, do you love me more than these?” He said to him, “Yes, Lord; you know that I have affection for you.” He said to him, “Feed my lambs.”"},{"strongs":"G5360","sense":"Brotherly love — the warm love of brothers and sisters, applied to the family of believers.","anchor":"Romans 12:10","en":"love","text":"In love of the brothers be tenderly affectionate to one another; in honor preferring one another;"},{"strongs":"G5387","sense":"Tenderly affectionate — the natural, family kind of love (στοργή) between relatives.","anchor":"Romans 12:10","en":"affectionate","text":"In love of the brothers be tenderly affectionate to one another; in honor preferring one another;"},{"strongs":"G5367","sense":"Lover of self — φίλος + αὐτός: love turned inward, the first mark of the last days.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:2","en":"lovers of self","text":"For men will be lovers of self, lovers of money, boastful, arrogant, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, unthankful, unholy,"},{"strongs":"G5366","sense":"Lover of money — φίλος + ἄργυρος, silver: love fastened on wealth.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:2","en":"lovers of money","text":"For men will be lovers of self, lovers of money, boastful, arrogant, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, unthankful, unholy,"},{"strongs":"G865","sense":"Not a lover of good — hostile to what is good; love withdrawn from goodness.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:3","en":"not lovers of good","text":"without natural affection, unforgiving, slanderers, without self-control, fierce, not lovers of good,"},{"strongs":"G5369","sense":"Lover of pleasure — φίλος + ἡδονή: love that lives for enjoyment.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:4","en":"lovers of pleasure","text":"traitors, headstrong, conceited, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God;"},{"strongs":"G5377","sense":"Lover of God — the love that should rule all others, and the one the last days replace with pleasure.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:4","en":"lovers of God","text":"traitors, headstrong, conceited, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God;"},{"strongs":"G5358","sense":"‘Lover of good’ — φίλος + ἀγαθός: one who delights in goodness and good people. The opposite of ἀφιλάγαθος (2 Timothy 3:3).","anchor":"Titus 1:8","en":"lover of good","text":"but given to hospitality, a lover of good, sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled;"},{"strongs":"G5382","sense":"‘Lover of strangers’ — φίλος + ξένος: hospitable, opening home and table to guests.","anchor":"Titus 1:8","en":"hospitality","text":"but given to hospitality, a lover of good, sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled;"},{"strongs":"G5362","sense":"‘Husband-loving’ — φίλος + ἀνήρ: warm, devoted affection of a wife for her husband.","anchor":"Titus 2:4","en":"love their husbands","text":"that they may train the young wives to love their husbands, to love their children,"},{"strongs":"G5388","sense":"‘Child-loving’ — φίλος + τέκνον: a parent’s tender affection for their children.","anchor":"Titus 2:4","en":"love their children","text":"that they may train the young wives to love their husbands, to love their children,"},{"strongs":"G5363","sense":"‘Love of humankind’ (the root of ‘philanthropy’): kindness toward people as people. Titus 3:4 uses it of God Himself.","anchor":"Titus 3:4","en":"love toward mankind","text":"But when the kindness of God our Savior and his love toward mankind appeared,"},{"strongs":"G5381","sense":"‘Love of strangers’ — hospitality: opening home and table to guests (Hebrews 13:2; the noun of φιλόξενος).","anchor":"Hebrews 13:2","en":"hospitality","text":"Don’t forget to show hospitality to strangers, for in doing so, some have entertained angels without knowing it."},{"strongs":"G866","sense":"‘Not a lover of money’ — free from greed; the opposite of φιλάργυρος.","anchor":"Hebrews 13:5","en":"love of money","text":"Be free from the love of money, content with such things as you have, for he has said, “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you.”"}]},{"id":"know","name":"Know & Knowledge","lang":"Greek","english":["know","knowledge"],"intro":"‘Know’ in English can mean knowing a fact or knowing a person. Greek usually keeps these apart. 1 Corinthians 8:1 has both οἶδα (‘we know’) and γνῶσις (‘knowledge’) in one sentence.","members":[{"strongs":"G1097","sense":"To come to know by experience and relationship — knowing a person, not just facts about them.","anchor":"John 17:3","en":"know","text":"This is eternal life, that they should know you, the only true God, and him whom you sent, Jesus Christ."},{"strongs":"G1492","sense":"To know as settled fact, to perceive (Strong's files the past-tense ‘saw’ under this number too).","anchor":"Romans 8:28","en":"know","text":"We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, to those who are called according to his purpose."},{"strongs":"G1108","sense":"Knowledge — what is known; at Corinth, knowledge that puffed people up.","anchor":"1 Corinthians 8:1","en":"knowledge","text":"Now concerning things sacrificed to idols: We know that we all have knowledge. Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up."},{"strongs":"G1922","sense":"Full, precise knowledge — ἐπί (upon) + γνῶσις: knowledge that grips and transforms.","anchor":"Colossians 1:9","en":"knowledge","text":"For this cause, we also, since the day we heard this, don’t cease praying and making requests for you, that you may be filled with the knowledge of his will in all spiritual wisdom and understanding,"},{"strongs":"G1987","sense":"To know well, understand, be acquainted with — Abraham went out ‘not knowing’ where he was going (Hebrews 11:8).","anchor":"Hebrews 11:8","en":"knowing","text":"By faith, Abraham, when he was called, obeyed to go out to the place which he was to receive for an inheritance. He went out, not knowing where he went."},{"strongs":"G1921","sense":"To know fully, recognise — the verb of ἐπίγνωσις: knowledge that grasps and acknowledges.","anchor":"1 Timothy 4:3","en":"know","text":"forbidding marriage and commanding to abstain from foods which God created to be received with thanksgiving by those who believe and know the truth."}]},{"id":"word","name":"Word & Utterance","lang":"Greek","english":["word"],"intro":"Both λόγος and ῥῆμα are translated ‘word’. The sword of the Spirit in Ephesians 6:17 is the ῥῆμα of God — the word spoken.","members":[{"strongs":"G3056","sense":"Word, message, reasoned account — and Jesus Himself, the Word.","anchor":"John 1:1","en":"Word","text":"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God."},{"strongs":"G4487","sense":"A spoken word, an utterance — a particular saying that proceeds out of a mouth.","anchor":"Ephesians 6:17","en":"word","text":"And take the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God;"}]},{"id":"life","name":"Life & Soul","lang":"Greek","english":["life","live","soul"],"intro":"‘Life’ in English Bibles can stand for three Greek words: life itself, the means of living, and the individual soul. John 10:10–11 uses two of them a sentence apart.","members":[{"strongs":"G2222","sense":"Life itself — the life that comes from God; ‘eternal life’.","anchor":"John 10:10","en":"life","text":"The thief only comes to steal, kill, and destroy. I came that they may have life, and may have it abundantly."},{"strongs":"G979","sense":"Livelihood, the means and course of earthly life — possessions, lifestyle.","anchor":"1 John 2:16","en":"life","text":"For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, isn’t the Father’s, but is the world’s."},{"strongs":"G5590","sense":"Soul — the individual self, the person's own life (laid down by the Good Shepherd).","anchor":"John 10:11","en":"life","text":"I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep."},{"strongs":"G2198","sense":"To live — the verb of ζωή: to be alive, with the life that comes from God.","anchor":"Hebrews 12:9","en":"live","text":"Furthermore, we had the fathers of our flesh to chasten us, and we paid them respect. Shall we not much rather be in subjection to the Father of spirits, and live?"}]},{"id":"new","name":"New","lang":"Greek","english":["new"],"intro":"Greek has one word for ‘new in kind’ and another for ‘new in time’. ἀνακαίνωσις, ‘renewal’, is built on καινός: the Spirit makes us new in kind, not merely recent. Hebrews calls the covenant ‘new’ with both words: καινός (8:8, new in kind) and νέος (12:24, freshly given). Hebrews 10:20 adds a third word, πρόσφατος, originally ‘freshly slain’: the new way opened through Christ’s sacrifice.","members":[{"strongs":"G2537","sense":"New in quality — fresh, unprecedented, of a different kind (a new creation).","anchor":"2 Corinthians 5:17","en":"new","text":"Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. Behold, all things have become new."},{"strongs":"G3501","sense":"New in time — recent, young (the new man, freshly come into being).","anchor":"Colossians 3:10","en":"new","text":"and have put on the new man, who is being renewed in knowledge after the image of his Creator,"},{"strongs":"G342","sense":"‘Renewal’ — ἀνά (again) + καινός: being made new in quality, renovated from within by the Holy Spirit (Titus 3:5), the renewing of the mind (Romans 12:2).","anchor":"Romans 12:2","en":"renewing","text":"Don’t be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, so that you may prove what is the good, well-pleasing, and perfect will of God."},{"strongs":"G4372","sense":"‘Freshly slain’, then simply ‘fresh, recent’ — the ‘new and living way’ opened by Christ’s death (Hebrews 10:20).","anchor":"Hebrews 10:20","en":"new","text":"by the way which he dedicated for us, a new and living way, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh;"}]},{"id":"another","name":"Another & Different","lang":"Greek","english":["another","different"],"intro":"Galatians 1:6–7 uses both words: a ‘different’ (ἕτερος) gospel, which is not ‘another’ (ἄλλος) gospel at all.","members":[{"strongs":"G243","sense":"Another of the same kind — Jesus promises another Counselor like Himself.","anchor":"John 14:16","en":"another","text":"I will pray to the Father, and he will give you another Counselor, that he may be with you forever,—"},{"strongs":"G2087","sense":"Another of a different kind — something other, different in nature.","anchor":"Galatians 1:6","en":"different","text":"I marvel that you are so quickly deserting him who called you in the grace of Christ to a different “good news”;"},{"strongs":"G2085","sense":"To teach differently — teaching of a different kind (ἕτερος) from the apostolic gospel.","anchor":"1 Timothy 1:3","en":"different","text":"As I urged you when I was going into Macedonia, stay at Ephesus that you might command certain men not to teach a different doctrine,"}]},{"id":"world","name":"World, Age & Earth","lang":"Greek","english":["world","earth"],"intro":"‘World’ in English Bibles can mean the created order, humanity, the system opposed to God, an age, or the inhabited lands. Romans 12:2's ‘this world’ is αἰών — this age.","members":[{"strongs":"G2889","sense":"The ordered world — creation, humanity, and often the world-system that rejects God.","anchor":"1 John 2:15","en":"world","text":"Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world. If anyone loves the world, the Father’s love isn’t in him."},{"strongs":"G165","sense":"The age — the present era with its values and spirit.","anchor":"Romans 12:2","en":"world","text":"Don’t be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, so that you may prove what is the good, well-pleasing, and perfect will of God."},{"strongs":"G3625","sense":"The inhabited earth — the civilised, populated world (often the Roman Empire).","anchor":"Luke 2:1","en":"world","text":"Now in those days, a decree went out from Caesar Augustus that all the world should be enrolled."},{"strongs":"G1093","sense":"Earth, land, ground — the physical earth or a territory.","anchor":"Matthew 5:5","en":"earth","text":"Blessed are the gentle, for they shall inherit the earth."}]},{"id":"servant","name":"Servant","lang":"Greek","english":["servant","servants"],"intro":"‘Servant’ can translate five Greek words, each describing a different relationship of service.","members":[{"strongs":"G1401","sense":"Bondservant, slave — one who belongs wholly to a master.","anchor":"Romans 1:1","en":"servant","text":"Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle, set apart for the Good News of God,"},{"strongs":"G1249","sense":"Servant in action — one who serves others, a minister (the root of ‘deacon’).","anchor":"Mark 10:43","en":"servant","text":"But it shall not be so among you, but whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant."},{"strongs":"G5257","sense":"Assistant, attendant — originally an ‘under-rower’ taking orders from above.","anchor":"1 Corinthians 4:1","en":"servants","text":"So let a man think of us as Christ’s servants, and stewards of God’s mysteries."},{"strongs":"G3610","sense":"Household servant — a domestic who lives in the master's house.","anchor":"Luke 16:13","en":"servant","text":"No servant can serve two masters, for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to one, and despise the other. You aren’t able to serve God and Mammon.”"},{"strongs":"G3011","sense":"Public or sacred servant — one who carries out a priestly or public service.","anchor":"Romans 15:16","en":"servant","text":"that I should be a servant of Christ Jesus to the Gentiles, serving as a priest of the Good News of God, that the offering up of the Gentiles might be made acceptable, sanctified by the Holy Spirit."},{"strongs":"G2324","sense":"An honoured attendant — a trusted servant in a household, the title God gave Moses (Hebrews 3:5). Contrasted with the Son who is over the house.","anchor":"Hebrews 3:5","en":"servant","text":"Moses indeed was faithful in all his house as a servant, for a testimony of those things which were afterward to be spoken,"},{"strongs":"G3000","sense":"To serve in worship — religious service offered to God (or to what stands in His place). Different from δουλεύω (to serve as a slave) and διακονέω (to serve practically).","anchor":"Hebrews 8:5","en":"serve","text":"who serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things, even as Moses was warned by God when he was about to make the tabernacle, for he said, “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain.”"},{"strongs":"G1247","sense":"To serve, wait on, minister to — the verb of διάκονος: practical service of others.","anchor":"2 Timothy 1:18","en":"served","text":"(the Lord grant to him to find the Lord’s mercy in that day); and in how many things he served at Ephesus, you know very well."},{"strongs":"G1248","sense":"Service, ministry — the practical help of a διάκονος.","anchor":"Hebrews 1:14","en":"service","text":"Aren’t they all serving spirits, sent out to do service for the sake of those who will inherit salvation?"},{"strongs":"G1398","sense":"To serve as a slave — the verb of δοῦλος: to be ruled by a master, whether God or desires.","anchor":"Titus 3:3","en":"serving","text":"For we were also once foolish, disobedient, deceived, serving various lusts and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, and hating one another."},{"strongs":"G1402","sense":"To enslave — to be made a δοῦλος, owned by something (Titus 2:3, ‘enslaved to much wine’).","anchor":"Titus 2:3","en":"enslaved","text":"and that older women likewise be reverent in behavior, not slanderers nor enslaved to much wine, teachers of that which is good;"},{"strongs":"G1397","sense":"Slavery, bondage — the condition of a δοῦλος (fear of death as bondage, Hebrews 2:15).","anchor":"Hebrews 2:15","en":"bondage","text":"and might deliver all of them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage."},{"strongs":"G2999","sense":"Worship-service — the noun of λατρεύω: the ordered service offered to God.","anchor":"Hebrews 9:1","en":"divine service","text":"Now indeed even the first covenant had ordinances of divine service, and an earthly sanctuary."}]},{"id":"work","name":"Work, Labour & Toil","lang":"Greek","english":["work","works","labor","toil","travail","working","busybodies","laborer"],"intro":"English ‘work’ and ‘labour’ cover several Greek words. 1 Thessalonians 1:3 sets ἔργον (work) beside κόπος (labour to the point of exhaustion), and 2:9 adds μόχθος (hardship) and ἐργάζομαι (working). Revelation 2:2 praises ‘works’ and ‘toil’ in a church that had left its first love.","members":[{"strongs":"G2041","sense":"Work, deed — an action or task, what a person does (the root of ‘ergonomics’).","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 1:3","en":"work","text":"remembering without ceasing your work of faith and labor of love and perseverance of hope in our Lord Jesus Christ, before our God and Father."},{"strongs":"G2873","sense":"Labour, toil — hard work that wears you out; effort to the point of weariness.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 1:3","en":"labor","text":"remembering without ceasing your work of faith and labor of love and perseverance of hope in our Lord Jesus Christ, before our God and Father."},{"strongs":"G2872","sense":"To labour, to toil to weariness — the verb of κόπος, used of those who work hard in ministry.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 5:12","en":"labor","text":"But we beg you, brothers, to know those who labor among you, and are over you in the Lord, and admonish you,"},{"strongs":"G3449","sense":"Hardship, travail — exhausting toil with difficulty and struggle.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 2:9","en":"travail","text":"For you remember, brothers, our labor and travail; for working night and day, that we might not burden any of you, we preached to you the Good News of God."},{"strongs":"G2040","sense":"Labourer, worker — one who works for wages; used of gospel workers and teachers.","anchor":"Luke 10:7","en":"laborer","text":"Remain in that same house, eating and drinking the things they give, for the laborer is worthy of his wages. Don’t go from house to house."},{"strongs":"G2038","sense":"To work — to do a job, to earn a living by labour.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 4:11","en":"work","text":"and that you make it your ambition to lead a quiet life, and to do your own business, and to work with your own hands, even as we instructed you;"},{"strongs":"G4020","sense":"To work around — to bustle about in other people's affairs instead of doing one's own work; to be a busybody.","anchor":"2 Thessalonians 3:11","en":"busybodies","text":"For we hear of some who walk among you in rebellion, who don’t work at all, but are busybodies."},{"strongs":"G4021","sense":"Busybody — ‘working around’, meddling in others' affairs; the adjective of περιεργάζομαι.","anchor":"1 Timothy 5:13","en":"busybodies","text":"Besides, they also learn to be idle, going about from house to house. Not only idle, but also gossips and busybodies, saying things which they ought not."}]},{"id":"sin","name":"Sin, Transgression & Debt","lang":"Greek","english":["sin","sins","transgression","transgressions","trespass","lawlessness","debts","unrighteousness"],"intro":"The New Testament describes sin from several angles: missing the mark, a false step, crossing a line, living without law, and owing a debt. Ephesians 2:1 uses two of these words together.","members":[{"strongs":"G266","sense":"Missing the mark — falling short of God's standard; the general word for sin.","anchor":"Romans 6:23","en":"sin","text":"For the wages of sin is death, but the free gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord."},{"strongs":"G3900","sense":"A false step, a fall beside the path (often translated ‘trespass’ or ‘transgression’).","anchor":"Ephesians 2:1","en":"transgressions","text":"You were made alive when you were dead in transgressions and sins,"},{"strongs":"G3847","sense":"Transgression — stepping over a known line, breaking a stated command.","anchor":"Galatians 3:19","en":"transgressions","text":"Then why is there the law? It was added because of transgressions, until the offspring should come to whom the promise has been made. It was ordained through angels by the hand of a mediator."},{"strongs":"G458","sense":"Lawlessness — living as if no law applied; rebellion against God's rule.","anchor":"1 John 3:4","en":"lawlessness","text":"Everyone who sins also commits lawlessness. Sin is lawlessness."},{"strongs":"G93","sense":"Unrighteousness, injustice — what is not right before God or toward people; ‘All unrighteousness is sin’.","anchor":"1 John 5:17","en":"unrighteousness","text":"All unrighteousness is sin, and there is a sin not leading to death."},{"strongs":"G3783","sense":"Debt — something owed; sin pictured as an unpaid debt to God.","anchor":"Matthew 6:12","en":"debts","text":"Forgive us our debts, as we also forgive our debtors."},{"strongs":"G3876","sense":"‘Mis-hearing’ — παρά + ἀκούω: refusing to listen, disobedience that begins with not hearing. Paired with παράβασις (stepping over the line) in Hebrews 2:2.","anchor":"Hebrews 2:2","en":"disobedience","text":"For if the word spoken through angels proved steadfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just recompense;"},{"strongs":"G51","sense":"A sin of ignorance — wrongdoing committed without full knowledge; the ‘errors’ the high priest atoned for on the Day of Atonement.","anchor":"Hebrews 9:7","en":"errors","text":"but into the second the high priest alone, once in the year, not without blood, which he offers for himself, and for the errors of the people."},{"strongs":"G264","sense":"To sin — the verb of ἁμαρτία: to miss the mark, and to keep on missing it.","anchor":"Hebrews 3:17","en":"sinned","text":"With whom was he displeased forty years? Wasn’t it with those who sinned, whose bodies fell in the wilderness?"},{"strongs":"G459","sense":"Lawless — without law, living as if God’s law did not exist; the adjective of ἀνομία.","anchor":"1 Timothy 1:9","en":"lawless","text":"as knowing this, that law is not made for a righteous person, but for the lawless and insubordinate, for the ungodly and sinners, for the unholy and profane, for murderers of fathers and murderers of mothers, for manslayers,"}]},{"id":"evil","name":"Evil & Malice","lang":"Greek","english":["evil","malice","wicked"],"intro":"English ‘evil’ hides several Greek words. κακός describes what is bad in itself — worthless, harmful in nature. πονηρός describes evil that is active and aggressive, actively doing harm; it is used of ‘the evil one’. 1 Thessalonians 5 uses both: do not repay κακός for κακός (5:15), and abstain from every form of πονηρός (5:22). φαῦλος adds a third shade: the worthless, paltry deed.","members":[{"strongs":"G2556","sense":"Bad, evil in nature — worthless, harmful; what is wrong in itself.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 5:15","en":"evil","text":"See that no one returns evil for evil to anyone, but always follow after that which is good, for one another, and for all."},{"strongs":"G4190","sense":"Evil in action — malicious, actively doing harm; ‘the evil one’.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 5:22","en":"evil","text":"Abstain from every form of evil."},{"strongs":"G2549","sense":"Malice — badness of heart, ill will that wants harm for others.","anchor":"Colossians 3:8","en":"malice","text":"but now you also put them all away: anger, wrath, malice, slander, and shameful speaking out of your mouth."},{"strongs":"G5337","sense":"‘Worthless, base’ — the shabby, good-for-nothing kind of wrong: deeds that cannot stand the light (John 3:20) and accusations an opponent might make (Titus 2:8).","anchor":"John 3:20","en":"evil","text":"For everyone who does evil hates the light, and doesn’t come to the light, lest his works would be exposed."}]},{"id":"image","name":"Image, Form & Likeness","lang":"Greek","english":["image","form","likeness"],"intro":"English ‘image’ and ‘form’ hide several Greek words. εἰκών is a true image or portrait that shares in what it shows (Colossians 1:15); χαρακτήρ is the exact imprint a seal or die leaves (Hebrews 1:3). μορφή is the form that expresses what a thing really is (Philippians 2:6), while σχῆμα is outward appearance and ὁμοίωμα likeness. μόρφωσις can be a mere outward shape without the reality (2 Timothy 3:5).","members":[{"strongs":"G1504","sense":"Image, portrait — a likeness that truly represents and shares in its original.","anchor":"Colossians 1:15","en":"image","text":"who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation."},{"strongs":"G5481","sense":"Exact imprint — the mark a seal or stamp leaves, matching it line for line.","anchor":"Hebrews 1:3","en":"image","text":"His Son is the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power, who, when he had by himself purified us of our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high;"},{"strongs":"G3444","sense":"Form — the outward expression of an inner, essential nature.","anchor":"Philippians 2:6","en":"form","text":"who, existing in the form of God, didn’t consider equality with God a thing to be grasped,"},{"strongs":"G3667","sense":"Likeness — resemblance, being made like something.","anchor":"Philippians 2:7","en":"likeness","text":"but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, being made in the likeness of men."},{"strongs":"G4976","sense":"Outward fashion, appearance — the way something presents to the eye.","anchor":"Philippians 2:8","en":"form","text":"And being found in human form, he humbled himself, becoming obedient to death, yes, the death of the cross."},{"strongs":"G3446","sense":"Outward shape — a form that may lack the reality (a ‘form of godliness’).","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:5","en":"form","text":"holding a form of godliness, but having denied its power. Turn away from these, also."}]},{"id":"pattern","name":"Pattern, Copy & Shadow","lang":"Greek","english":["pattern","copy","copies","shadow","representations","symbol","foreshadowing","example"],"intro":"Hebrews explains the old covenant with a family of ‘picture’ words. τύπος is the original pattern or stamp from which copies are made (the plan shown to Moses, 8:5) — and also a ‘type’ that foreshadows (Romans 5:14). ὑπόδειγμα is a copy or sketch (8:5; 9:23), and also an example to follow or avoid (4:11). σκιά is a shadow, cast by a reality not yet seen (8:5; 10:1). ἀντίτυπον is a counterpart that corresponds to the original (9:24). παραβολή is a symbol, a comparison set alongside (9:9). Together they say: the tabernacle, priests and sacrifices were real, but they were pictures of Christ.","members":[{"strongs":"G5179","sense":"Pattern, original model — the stamp from which copies are made; also a type that foreshadows.","anchor":"Hebrews 8:5","en":"pattern","text":"who serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things, even as Moses was warned by God when he was about to make the tabernacle, for he said, “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain.”"},{"strongs":"G5262","sense":"Copy, sketch — a representation of the original; also an example.","anchor":"Hebrews 8:5","en":"copy","text":"who serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things, even as Moses was warned by God when he was about to make the tabernacle, for he said, “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain.”"},{"strongs":"G4639","sense":"Shadow — the outline cast by a reality not yet seen.","anchor":"Hebrews 8:5","en":"shadow","text":"who serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things, even as Moses was warned by God when he was about to make the tabernacle, for he said, “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain.”"},{"strongs":"G499","sense":"Counterpart — what corresponds to the original, a representation.","anchor":"Hebrews 9:24","en":"representations","text":"For Christ hasn’t entered into holy places made with hands, which are representations of the true, but into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us;"},{"strongs":"G3850","sense":"Symbol, comparison — something set alongside to illustrate.","anchor":"Hebrews 9:9","en":"symbol","text":"which is a symbol of the present age, where gifts and sacrifices are offered that are incapable, concerning the conscience, of making the worshiper perfect;"}]},{"id":"hold","name":"Hold Fast, Keep & Guard","lang":"Greek","english":["hold fast","hold tightly","keep","kept","guard","holding"],"intro":"English ‘hold fast’ and ‘keep’ hide several Greek words. κατέχω is to hold down, keep a firm grip so nothing slips (Hebrews 3:14; 10:23). κρατέω, from κράτος (strength), is to seize and hold with force (Hebrews 4:14). τηρέω is to keep watch over, preserve (2 Timothy 4:7 ‘I have kept the faith’). φυλάσσω is to guard like a sentry (1 Timothy 6:20). ἀντέχομαι is to cling to, hold on against opposition (Titus 1:9).","members":[{"strongs":"G2722","sense":"To hold down, keep a firm grip — not letting go of what you have.","anchor":"Hebrews 3:14","en":"hold fast","text":"For we have become partakers of Christ, if we hold fast the beginning of our confidence firm to the end:"},{"strongs":"G2902","sense":"To grasp with strength, seize and retain (from κράτος).","anchor":"Hebrews 4:14","en":"hold tightly","text":"Having then a great high priest, who has passed through the heavens, Jesus, the Son of God, let us hold tightly to our confession."},{"strongs":"G5083","sense":"To keep watch over, preserve, keep intact.","anchor":"2 Timothy 4:7","en":"kept","text":"I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith."},{"strongs":"G5442","sense":"To guard as a sentry — protect against loss or attack.","anchor":"1 Timothy 6:20","en":"guard","text":"Timothy, guard that which is committed to you, turning away from the empty chatter and oppositions of what is falsely called knowledge;"},{"strongs":"G472","sense":"To cling to, hold on firmly against pressure.","anchor":"Titus 1:9","en":"holding","text":"holding to the faithful word which is according to the teaching, that he may be able to exhort in the sound doctrine, and to convict those who contradict him."}]},{"id":"offering","name":"Sacrifice, Offering & Gift","lang":"Greek","english":["sacrifice","offering","gifts","burnt offerings","offer"],"intro":"Hebrews uses a whole vocabulary of worship. θυσία is a sacrifice, usually an animal slain; προσφορά is an offering, the act of bringing something to God; ὁλοκαύτωμα is a whole burnt offering, entirely consumed; δῶρον is a gift. The verb προσφέρω, to bring near, to offer, is the priest’s work. Hebrews 10:5–8 gathers four of these words to show that all of them are fulfilled and ended in Christ’s one offering of His body.","members":[{"strongs":"G2378","sense":"A sacrifice — usually a slain animal, offered to God.","anchor":"Hebrews 10:5","en":"Sacrifice","text":"Therefore when he comes into the world, he says, “Sacrifice and offering you didn’t desire, but you prepared a body for me."},{"strongs":"G4376","sense":"An offering — what is brought near to God, the act of offering.","anchor":"Hebrews 10:5","en":"offering","text":"Therefore when he comes into the world, he says, “Sacrifice and offering you didn’t desire, but you prepared a body for me."},{"strongs":"G3646","sense":"A whole burnt offering — wholly consumed on the altar.","anchor":"Hebrews 10:6","en":"whole burnt offerings","text":"You had no pleasure in whole burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin."},{"strongs":"G1435","sense":"A gift — an offering presented to God.","anchor":"Hebrews 5:1","en":"gifts","text":"For every high priest, being taken from among men, is appointed for men in things pertaining to God, that he may offer both gifts and sacrifices for sins."},{"strongs":"G4374","sense":"To bring near, to offer — the priest’s action.","anchor":"Hebrews 5:1","en":"offer","text":"For every high priest, being taken from among men, is appointed for men in things pertaining to God, that he may offer both gifts and sacrifices for sins."},{"strongs":"G399","sense":"To carry up, to offer up on the altar — used of Christ who ‘offered up himself’ once for all (Hebrews 7:27).","anchor":"Hebrews 7:27","en":"offer up","text":"who doesn’t need, like those high priests, to offer up sacrifices daily, first for his own sins, and then for the sins of the people. For he did this once for all, when he offered up himself."}]},{"id":"godliness","name":"Godliness & Ungodliness","lang":"Greek","english":["godliness","godly","ungodly","ungodliness","piety"],"intro":"‘Godliness’ is the key word of 1 Timothy. English uses one word for two Greek ones: εὐσέβεια (well-directed reverence, devotion to God lived out in daily life) and the rarer θεοσέβεια (reverence for God). The opposite is ἀσέβεια / ἀσεβής — the absence of reverence, life lived as if God did not matter. Hebrews adds εὐλάβεια, reverent awe (‘godly fear’ in 5:7).","members":[{"strongs":"G2150","sense":"Godliness — reverent devotion to God expressed in a whole way of life; the key word of the pastoral letters.","anchor":"1 Timothy 6:6","en":"godliness","text":"But godliness with contentment is great gain."},{"strongs":"G2151","sense":"To show piety, to act reverently — godliness put into practice, beginning at home with one's own family.","anchor":"1 Timothy 5:4","en":"piety","text":"But if any widow has children or grandchildren, let them learn first to show piety towards their own family, and to repay their parents, for this is acceptable in the sight of God."},{"strongs":"G2153","sense":"Godly (adverb) — living reverently toward God, the way of life that draws persecution.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:12","en":"godly","text":"Yes, and all who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus will suffer persecution."},{"strongs":"G2317","sense":"Reverence for God, God-fearing piety (used only once in the New Testament).","anchor":"1 Timothy 2:10","en":"godliness","text":"but (which becomes women professing godliness) with good works."},{"strongs":"G765","sense":"Ungodly — without reverence for God; living as if God did not matter.","anchor":"1 Timothy 1:9","en":"ungodly","text":"as knowing this, that law is not made for a righteous person, but for the lawless and insubordinate, for the ungodly and sinners, for the unholy and profane, for murderers of fathers and murderers of mothers, for manslayers,"},{"strongs":"G763","sense":"Ungodliness — irreverence toward God; the noun of ἀσεβής, life without regard for God.","anchor":"2 Timothy 2:16","en":"ungodliness","text":"But shun empty chatter, for it will go further in ungodliness,"},{"strongs":"G2124","sense":"Reverent awe — careful, God-fearing reverence. Jesus was heard ‘for his godly fear’ (Hebrews 5:7); believers serve God ‘with reverence’ (12:28).","anchor":"Hebrews 5:7","en":"godly fear","text":"He, in the days of his flesh, having offered up prayers and petitions with strong crying and tears to him who was able to save him from death, and having been heard for his godly fear,"},{"strongs":"G2125","sense":"To act with reverent caution, to be moved by godly fear — the verb of εὐλάβεια (Noah, Hebrews 11:7).","anchor":"Hebrews 11:7","en":"godly fear","text":"By faith, Noah, being warned about things not yet seen, moved with godly fear, prepared a ship for the saving of his house, through which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is according to faith."}]},{"id":"self-control","name":"Sober, Sound-Minded & Self-Controlled","lang":"Greek","english":["sober","sober minded","soberly","self-control","self-controlled","temperate","right mind","reasonableness","train"],"intro":"English ‘sober’ and ‘self-control’ hide three Greek roots. νήφω / νηφάλιος is literally not drunk — clear-headed, alert. The σώφρων family (‘a saved, sound mind’) is sanity and moderation: thinking straight about God, self and others; the healed demoniac of Mark 5:15 is σωφρονοῦντα, ‘in his right mind’. ἐγκράτεια / ἐγκρατής is mastery, strength held over one’s own desires. Titus 1:8 puts σώφρων and ἐγκρατής side by side, and Titus 2 uses the σώφρων words for every age group.","members":[{"strongs":"G3525","sense":"To be sober, not intoxicated — figuratively, clear-headed and alert, especially in watching for the Lord.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 5:6","en":"sober","text":"so then let’s not sleep, as the rest do, but let’s watch and be sober."},{"strongs":"G3524","sense":"Sober, temperate — free from the haze of wine and excess; circumspect.","anchor":"1 Timothy 3:2","en":"temperate","text":"The overseer therefore must be without reproach, the husband of one wife, temperate, sensible, modest, hospitable, good at teaching;"},{"strongs":"G4998","sense":"Of sound mind — sensible, self-controlled, moderate: the mind kept safe and whole.","anchor":"Titus 1:8","en":"sober minded","text":"but given to hospitality, a lover of good, sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled;"},{"strongs":"G4993","sense":"To be of sound mind — sane, sensible; used of the healed demoniac ‘in his right mind’.","anchor":"Mark 5:15","en":"right mind","text":"They came to Jesus, and saw him who had been possessed by demons sitting, clothed, and in his right mind, even him who had the legion; and they were afraid."},{"strongs":"G4996","sense":"With a sound mind — living sensibly and with restraint.","anchor":"Titus 2:12","en":"soberly","text":"instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age;"},{"strongs":"G4997","sense":"Soundness of mind — good sense, sober reasonableness.","anchor":"Acts 26:25","en":"reasonableness","text":"But he said, “I am not crazy, most excellent Festus, but boldly declare words of truth and reasonableness."},{"strongs":"G4995","sense":"Self-discipline — a sound, disciplined mind given by the Spirit.","anchor":"2 Timothy 1:7","en":"self-control","text":"For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control."},{"strongs":"G4994","sense":"To make sound-minded — to train, coach or bring someone to good sense.","anchor":"Titus 2:4","en":"train","text":"that they may train the young wives to love their husbands, to love their children,"},{"strongs":"G1466","sense":"Mastery over oneself — strength that holds desires in check (the Spirit’s fruit).","anchor":"Galatians 5:23","en":"self-control","text":"gentleness, and self-control. Against such things there is no law."},{"strongs":"G1468","sense":"Self-mastered — having one’s appetites under control.","anchor":"Titus 1:8","en":"self-controlled","text":"but given to hospitality, a lover of good, sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled;"}]},{"id":"discipline","name":"Discipline, Training & Chastening","lang":"Greek","english":["discipline","chastening","chastens","instructing","punished","exercised","reproved","scourges"],"intro":"English varies its words for one Greek family. παιδεία / παιδεύω (from παῖς, a child) is the whole upbringing of a child — teaching, training and correction. The WEB renders it ‘chastening’ (Hebrews 12:5), ‘discipline’ (12:7), ‘punished’ (12:10) and ‘instructing’ (Titus 2:12, where grace is the trainer). γυμνάζω is athletic training (‘exercised’, 5:14; 12:11). ἐλέγχω is reproof that exposes and corrects. μαστιγόω, to scourge, is the hardest form. Hebrews 12 says God’s discipline is the proof of sonship and the path to holiness.","members":[{"strongs":"G3809","sense":"Child-training — instruction and correction by a loving father; ‘discipline’.","anchor":"Hebrews 12:7","en":"discipline","text":"It is for discipline that you endure. God deals with you as with children, for what son is there whom his father doesn’t discipline?"},{"strongs":"G3811","sense":"To train a child — to teach, guide, correct; grace itself does this (Titus 2:12).","anchor":"Hebrews 12:6","en":"chastens","text":"For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he receives.”"},{"strongs":"G1128","sense":"To train like an athlete — exercise by repeated practice.","anchor":"Hebrews 12:11","en":"exercised","text":"All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous; yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby."},{"strongs":"G1651","sense":"To reprove — expose a fault in order to correct it.","anchor":"Hebrews 12:5","en":"reproved","text":"and you have forgotten the exhortation which reasons with you as with children, “My son, don’t take lightly the chastening of the Lord, nor faint when you are reproved by him;"},{"strongs":"G3146","sense":"To scourge, whip — severe correction.","anchor":"Hebrews 12:6","en":"scourges","text":"For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he receives.”"}]},{"id":"perfect","name":"Perfect, Complete & Mature","lang":"Greek","english":["perfect","perfection","perfecter","complete","full grown"],"intro":"English ‘perfect’ usually means flawless, but the Greek τέλειος family means reaching the goal (τέλος): complete, mature, fully grown. Hebrews uses it again and again: Christ was ‘made perfect’ — fully qualified as Saviour — through suffering (2:10), and is the ‘perfecter’ of faith (12:2). The law could not bring τελείωσις (7:11). A different root, καταρτίζω / ἄρτιος, means fitted, equipped, mended; ὁλόκληρος means whole in every part.","members":[{"strongs":"G5048","sense":"To bring to the goal — complete, make fully qualified, bring to maturity.","anchor":"Hebrews 2:10","en":"perfect","text":"For it became him, for whom are all things, and through whom are all things, in bringing many children to glory, to make the author of their salvation perfect through sufferings."},{"strongs":"G5046","sense":"Complete, mature, full-grown — having reached the goal.","anchor":"Matthew 5:48","en":"perfect","text":"Therefore you shall be perfect, just as your Father in heaven is perfect."},{"strongs":"G5047","sense":"Maturity, completeness — growing up in the faith.","anchor":"Hebrews 6:1","en":"perfection","text":"Therefore leaving the teaching of the first principles of Christ, let us press on to perfection—not laying again a foundation of repentance from dead works, of faith toward God,"},{"strongs":"G5050","sense":"Completion, bringing to the goal — what the old priesthood could not achieve.","anchor":"Hebrews 7:11","en":"perfection","text":"Now if there were perfection through the Levitical priesthood (for under it the people have received the law), what further need was there for another priest to arise after the order of Melchizedek, and not be called after the order of Aaron?"},{"strongs":"G5051","sense":"Completer — one who brings something to its goal (only in Hebrews 12:2).","anchor":"Hebrews 12:2","en":"perfecter","text":"looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith, who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising its shame, and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God."},{"strongs":"G2675","sense":"To fit out, mend, equip completely — like repairing nets or setting a bone.","anchor":"1 Peter 5:10","en":"perfect","text":"But may the God of all grace, who called you to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little while, perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you."},{"strongs":"G739","sense":"Fitted, fully capable — exactly suited for the task.","anchor":"2 Timothy 3:17","en":"complete","text":"that the man of God may be complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work."},{"strongs":"G3648","sense":"Whole in every part — nothing missing.","anchor":"James 1:4","en":"complete","text":"Let endurance have its perfect work, that you may be perfect and complete, lacking in nothing."}]},{"id":"author","name":"Author, Pioneer & Source","lang":"Greek","english":["author","Prince"],"intro":"The WEB uses ‘author’ for two different Greek words in Hebrews. ἀρχηγός (2:10; 12:2) is a pioneer or leader who goes first and opens the way, a founder (Peter calls Jesus ‘the Prince of life’ with it). αἴτιος (5:9) is the cause or source: Christ is the One from whom eternal salvation comes.","members":[{"strongs":"G747","sense":"Pioneer, trailblazer, leader who goes first and opens the way.","anchor":"Hebrews 2:10","en":"author","text":"For it became him, for whom are all things, and through whom are all things, in bringing many children to glory, to make the author of their salvation perfect through sufferings."},{"strongs":"G159","sense":"Cause, source — the one from whom something comes.","anchor":"Hebrews 5:9","en":"author","text":"Having been made perfect, he became to all of those who obey him the author of eternal salvation,"}]},{"id":"pray","name":"Prayer, Asking & Intercession","lang":"Greek","english":["pray","prayer","ask","petition","intercession","intercessions","thanksgiving"],"intro":"Several Greek words lie behind ‘pray’ and ‘ask’. In John 14:16 the WEB's ‘I will pray to the Father’ is ἐρωτάω — to ask, as one who speaks to the Father as an equal.","members":[{"strongs":"G4336","sense":"To pray to God — prayer as worship and communion, used only of prayer to God.","anchor":"Matthew 6:6","en":"pray","text":"But you, when you pray, enter into your inner room, and having shut your door, pray to your Father who is in secret, and your Father who sees in secret will reward you openly."},{"strongs":"G4335","sense":"Prayer (noun) — prayer as worship and communion with God, the general word for prayer.","anchor":"Philippians 4:6","en":"prayer","text":"In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God."},{"strongs":"G1783","sense":"Intercession — approaching someone, especially a king, to plead on behalf of others.","anchor":"1 Timothy 2:1","en":"intercessions","text":"I exhort therefore, first of all, that petitions, prayers, intercessions, and givings of thanks, be made for all men:"},{"strongs":"G2169","sense":"Thanksgiving — grateful prayer, giving thanks to God (the root of ‘Eucharist’).","anchor":"Philippians 4:6","en":"thanksgiving","text":"In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God."},{"strongs":"G1162","sense":"Petition — a request rising out of a specific need.","anchor":"Philippians 4:6","en":"petition","text":"In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God."},{"strongs":"G1189","sense":"To beg, to plead — the verb of δέησις: earnest, specific asking out of need.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 3:10","en":"praying","text":"night and day praying exceedingly that we may see your face, and may perfect that which is lacking in your faith?"},{"strongs":"G154","sense":"To ask for something — a request from a lesser to a greater.","anchor":"Matthew 7:7","en":"Ask","text":"“Ask, and it will be given you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and it will be opened for you."},{"strongs":"G2065","sense":"To ask, request — on equal footing; used of Jesus asking the Father.","anchor":"John 14:16","en":"pray","text":"I will pray to the Father, and he will give you another Counselor, that he may be with you forever,—"},{"strongs":"G1793","sense":"To intercede — to meet with someone on another's behalf.","anchor":"Hebrews 7:25","en":"intercession","text":"Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through him, seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them."},{"strongs":"G2428","sense":"A suppliant’s plea — originally the olive branch held out by someone begging for mercy; earnest entreaty.","anchor":"Hebrews 5:7","en":"petitions","text":"He, in the days of his flesh, having offered up prayers and petitions with strong crying and tears to him who was able to save him from death, and having been heard for his godly fear,"}]},{"id":"good","name":"Good","lang":"Greek","english":["good"],"intro":"Greek distinguishes what is good in its effect from what is good and beautiful to look at.","members":[{"strongs":"G18","sense":"Good in nature and effect — beneficial, doing good.","anchor":"Romans 8:28","en":"good","text":"We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, to those who are called according to his purpose."},{"strongs":"G2570","sense":"Good and beautiful — noble, excellent, attractive; the Good Shepherd.","anchor":"John 10:11","en":"good","text":"I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep."},{"strongs":"G14","sense":"To do good, to work what is good — ἀγαθός put into action, especially generosity.","anchor":"1 Timothy 6:18","en":"good","text":"that they do good, that they be rich in good works, that they be ready to distribute, willing to share;"},{"strongs":"G2567","sense":"‘Teacher of what is καλός’ — one whose life and words teach what is good and beautiful.","anchor":"Titus 2:3","en":"teachers of that which is good","text":"and that older women likewise be reverent in behavior, not slanderers nor enslaved to much wine, teachers of that which is good;"},{"strongs":"G2140","sense":"Doing good — εὖ + ποιέω: practical kindness, beneficence, one of the ‘sacrifices’ that please God (Hebrews 13:16).","anchor":"Hebrews 13:16","en":"doing good","text":"But don’t forget to be doing good and sharing, for with such sacrifices God is well pleased."}]},{"id":"see","name":"See, Look & Behold","lang":"Greek","english":["see","saw","look"],"intro":"John 20:5–8 uses three different verbs that the WEB translates ‘saw’: a glance, a careful gaze, and seeing that leads to understanding — ‘he saw and believed’. Hebrews 2:8–9 puts ὁράω and βλέπω side by side.","members":[{"strongs":"G991","sense":"To look, to see with the eyes — the simple act of seeing.","anchor":"John 20:5","en":"saw","text":"Stooping and looking in, he saw the linen cloths lying, yet he didn’t enter in."},{"strongs":"G2334","sense":"To gaze, observe carefully — watching with attention (the root of ‘theatre’).","anchor":"John 20:6","en":"saw","text":"Then Simon Peter came, following him, and entered into the tomb. He saw the linen cloths lying,"},{"strongs":"G1492","sense":"To see and perceive — seeing that understands (Strong's files ‘saw’ and ‘know’ under one number).","anchor":"John 20:8","en":"saw","text":"So then the other disciple who came first to the tomb also entered in, and he saw and believed."},{"strongs":"G2300","sense":"To behold — looking intently and taking in what is seen.","anchor":"John 1:14","en":"saw","text":"The Word became flesh, and lived among us. We saw his glory, such glory as of the one and only Son of the Father, full of grace and truth."},{"strongs":"G3708","sense":"To see with the eyes or the mind — to perceive, take in. Hebrews 2:8–9 sets it beside βλέπω: we do not yet ‘see’ (ὁράω) everything subject to Him, but we do ‘see’ (βλέπω) Jesus.","anchor":"Hebrews 2:8","en":"see","text":"You have put all things in subjection under his feet.” For in that he subjected all things to him, he left nothing that is not subject to him. But now we don’t see all things subjected to him, yet."},{"strongs":"G872","sense":"To look away from everything else and fix the eyes on one object — ‘looking to Jesus’ (Hebrews 12:2).","anchor":"Hebrews 12:2","en":"looking","text":"looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith, who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising its shame, and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God."}]},{"id":"appear","name":"Appear, Reveal & Manifest","lang":"Greek","english":["appear","appeared","appearing","revealed"],"intro":"English ‘appear’ and ‘reveal’ cover several Greek words. ἐπιφαίνω / ἐπιφάνεια is a shining-forth, like dawn breaking or a king’s arrival: Titus uses it for both comings of Christ (2:11, 13; 3:4). φανερόω is to make visible what was hidden; φαίνω, to shine or be seen; ὤφθη (from ὁράω / ὀπτάνομαι), ‘was seen’, is used of the risen Lord’s appearances; ἀποκαλύπτω is to unveil, to take the cover off. Hebrews 9:24–28 uses three of them for Christ’s three appearings: φανερόω (He ‘has been revealed’ once, v. 26), ἐμφανίζω (He now appears before God for us, v. 24) and ὤφθη/ὁράω (He ‘will appear’ a second time, v. 28).","members":[{"strongs":"G2014","sense":"To shine forth upon — to dawn, to appear in a burst of light.","anchor":"Titus 2:11","en":"appeared","text":"For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men,"},{"strongs":"G2015","sense":"A shining-forth, a glorious manifestation — used of Christ’s first and second comings.","anchor":"2 Timothy 1:10","en":"appearing","text":"but has now been revealed by the appearing of our Savior, Christ Jesus, who abolished death, and brought life and immortality to light through the Good News."},{"strongs":"G5319","sense":"To make visible, bring into the open what was hidden.","anchor":"Colossians 3:4","en":"revealed","text":"When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory."},{"strongs":"G5316","sense":"To shine; to appear, be seen.","anchor":"Matthew 1:20","en":"appeared","text":"But when he thought about these things, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying, “Joseph, son of David, don’t be afraid to take to yourself Mary, your wife, for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."},{"strongs":"G3700","sense":"To be seen — the word of the resurrection appearances: ‘he was seen by’.","anchor":"1 Corinthians 15:5","en":"appeared","text":"and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve."},{"strongs":"G601","sense":"To unveil, take the cover off — revelation of what only God can show.","anchor":"Romans 1:17","en":"revealed","text":"For in it is revealed God’s righteousness from faith to faith. As it is written, “But the righteous shall live by faith.”"},{"strongs":"G1718","sense":"To show oneself, appear openly before someone — Christ now appearing in God’s presence on our behalf (Hebrews 9:24).","anchor":"Hebrews 9:24","en":"appear","text":"For Christ hasn’t entered into holy places made with hands, which are representations of the true, but into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us;"},{"strongs":"G5324","sense":"To make visible, display — from φαίνω: the terrifying ‘appearance’ at Sinai (Hebrews 12:21).","anchor":"Hebrews 12:21","en":"appearance","text":"and so fearful was the appearance that Moses said, “I am terrified and trembling.”"}]},{"id":"rise","name":"Rise, Raise & Resurrection","lang":"Greek","english":["raise","raised","rise","rose","resurrection"],"intro":"Two Greek verbs lie behind ‘raise’ and ‘rise’. ἐγείρω pictures waking someone up; ἀνίστημι pictures standing up again. 1 Thessalonians uses both: God ‘raised’ Jesus (1:10), Jesus ‘rose’ (4:14) and the dead in Christ will ‘rise’ (4:16). The noun ἀνάστασις, resurrection, comes from the second.","members":[{"strongs":"G1453","sense":"To wake, to rouse, to raise — often God raising Jesus, as one wakes a sleeper.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 1:10","en":"raised","text":"and to wait for his Son from heaven, whom he raised from the dead—Jesus, who delivers us from the wrath to come."},{"strongs":"G450","sense":"To stand up, to rise again — the body standing up out of death.","anchor":"1 Thessalonians 4:14","en":"rose","text":"For if we believe that Jesus died and rose again, even so God will bring with him those who have fallen asleep in Jesus."},{"strongs":"G386","sense":"Resurrection — a standing up again from the dead.","anchor":"John 11:25","en":"resurrection","text":"Jesus said to her, “I am the resurrection and the life. He who believes in me will still live, even if he dies."}]},{"id":"sleep","name":"Sleep & Wakefulness","lang":"Greek","english":["sleep","asleep","sleeping","watch","wake"],"intro":"The New Testament uses two words for ‘sleep’. κοιμάω is the gentle word for believers who have died — they will wake. καθεύδω is ordinary sleep, and in 1 Thessalonians 5 the spiritual drowsiness of those who are not ready. Its opposite is γρηγορέω, to stay awake and watch. In 5:10 Paul uses ‘wake or sleep’ to mean living or having died.","members":[{"strongs":"G2837","sense":"To fall asleep — used of believers who have died, because death for them is temporary.","anchor":"John 11:11","en":"asleep","text":"He said these things, and after that, he said to them, “Our friend, Lazarus, has fallen asleep, but I am going so that I may awake him out of sleep.”"},{"strongs":"G2518","sense":"To sleep, lie down to rest — ordinary sleep; figuratively, spiritual drowsiness.","anchor":"Mark 5:39","en":"asleep","text":"When he had entered in, he said to them, “Why do you make an uproar and weep? The child is not dead, but is asleep.”"},{"strongs":"G1127","sense":"To stay awake, to watch — alert and ready; the opposite of spiritual sleep.","anchor":"Colossians 4:2","en":"watching","text":"Continue steadfastly in prayer, watching therein with thanksgiving;"},{"strongs":"G69","sense":"To be sleepless, keep awake — to stay alert and watchful, as leaders ‘watch on behalf of your souls’ (Hebrews 13:17).","anchor":"Hebrews 13:17","en":"watch","text":"Obey your leaders and submit to them, for they watch on behalf of your souls, as those who will give account, that they may do this with joy, and not with groaning, for that would be unprofitable for you."}]},{"id":"rest","name":"Rest & Relief","lang":"Greek","english":["rest","rested","Sabbath rest","relief"],"intro":"Hebrews 3–4 uses several words for ‘rest’. κατάπαυσις / καταπαύω is settling down to stay — the rest of Canaan and of God’s own rest. σαββατισμός (only in Hebrews 4:9) is a Sabbath-keeping, the rest that celebrates finished work. ἀνάπαυσις / ἀναπαύω is refreshment, a break from toil (Matthew 11:28–29). ἄνεσις is relief, the loosening of pressure.","members":[{"strongs":"G2663","sense":"A settling down, a place of rest — the promised land and God’s own rest.","anchor":"Hebrews 4:1","en":"rest","text":"Let us fear therefore, lest perhaps anyone of you should seem to have come short of a promise of entering into his rest."},{"strongs":"G2664","sense":"To bring to rest, to cause to settle; also to cease from work.","anchor":"Hebrews 4:8","en":"rest","text":"For if Joshua had given them rest, he would not have spoken afterward of another day."},{"strongs":"G4520","sense":"A Sabbath-keeping — the rest that remains for the people of God.","anchor":"Hebrews 4:9","en":"Sabbath rest","text":"There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God."},{"strongs":"G372","sense":"Refreshment, a pause from labour.","anchor":"Matthew 11:29","en":"rest","text":"Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart; and you will find rest for your souls."},{"strongs":"G373","sense":"To give rest, refresh; to take one’s rest.","anchor":"Matthew 11:28","en":"rest","text":"“Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest."},{"strongs":"G425","sense":"Relief — loosening, release from pressure or affliction.","anchor":"2 Thessalonians 1:7","en":"relief","text":"and to give relief to you who are afflicted with us, when the Lord Jesus is revealed from heaven with his mighty angels in flaming fire,"}]},{"id":"god-names","name":"Names and Titles of God","lang":"Hebrew","english":["Yahweh","LORD","God","Lord","Armies","Almighty"],"intro":"The Old Testament uses several names and titles for God, and English Bibles often hide them behind ‘LORD’, ‘Lord’ and ‘God’. The WEB writes the personal name as ‘Yahweh’.","members":[{"strongs":"H3068","sense":"Yahweh — God's personal covenant name (written ‘LORD’ in most English Bibles).","anchor":"Malachi 3:6","en":"Yahweh","text":"“For I, Yahweh, don’t change; therefore you, sons of Jacob, are not consumed."},{"strongs":"H430","sense":"Elohim — God, the mighty Creator; plural in form, used with singular verbs for the one true God.","anchor":"Zephaniah 3:17","en":"God","text":"Yahweh, your God, is among you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you in his love. He will rejoice over you with singing."},{"strongs":"H410","sense":"El — God, the Mighty One; often in compound titles (El Shaddai, El Elyon).","anchor":"Malachi 2:10","en":"God","text":"Don’t we all have one father? Hasn’t one God created us? Why do we deal treacherously every man against his brother, profaning the covenant of our fathers?"},{"strongs":"H136","sense":"Adonai — Lord, Master; God as sovereign owner.","anchor":"Zephaniah 1:7","en":"Lord","text":"Be silent at the presence of the Lord Yahweh, for the day of Yahweh is at hand. For Yahweh has prepared a sacrifice. He has consecrated his guests."},{"strongs":"H6635","sense":"Tsevaot — armies, hosts; ‘Yahweh of Armies’, commander of heaven's forces.","anchor":"Haggai 1:2","en":"Armies","text":"“This is what Yahweh of Armies says: These people say, ‘The time hasn’t yet come, the time for Yahweh’s house to be built.’”"},{"strongs":"H7706","sense":"Shaddai — the Almighty; God all-sufficient.","anchor":"Genesis 17:1","en":"Almighty","text":""}]},{"id":"sin-hebrew","name":"Sin, Iniquity & Transgression","lang":"Hebrew","english":["sin","iniquity","transgression","transgressions"],"intro":"Hebrew has three main words for wrongdoing, and Psalm 32:5 uses all three in one verse: sin, iniquity and transgressions.","members":[{"strongs":"H2403","sense":"Sin — missing the mark; also the sin offering that deals with it.","anchor":"Zechariah 13:1","en":"sin","text":"“In that day there will be a spring opened to David’s house and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, for sin and for uncleanness."},{"strongs":"H5771","sense":"Iniquity — twistedness, perversity, and the guilt it carries.","anchor":"Zechariah 3:4","en":"iniquity","text":"He answered and spoke to those who stood before him, saying, “Take the filthy garments off him.” To him he said, “Behold, I have caused your iniquity to pass from you, and I will clothe you with rich clothing.”"},{"strongs":"H6588","sense":"Transgression — rebellion, deliberate revolt against God's authority.","anchor":"Psalm 32:5","en":"transgressions","text":""}]},{"id":"love-hebrew","name":"Love, Kindness & Compassion","lang":"Hebrew","english":["love","loved","kindness","compassion","grace"],"intro":"Hebrew describes God's love from several angles: choosing love, covenant loyalty, womb-deep compassion and undeserved favour. Zechariah 7:9 puts kindness and compassion side by side.","members":[{"strongs":"H157","sense":"To love — to choose and hold dear (God's love for Israel).","anchor":"Malachi 1:2","en":"loved","text":"“I have loved you,” says Yahweh. Yet you say, “How have you loved us?” “Wasn’t Esau Jacob’s brother?” says Yahweh, “Yet I loved Jacob;"},{"strongs":"H160","sense":"Love (noun) — affection, the love God quietly rests in over His people.","anchor":"Zephaniah 3:17","en":"love","text":"Yahweh, your God, is among you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you in his love. He will rejoice over you with singing."},{"strongs":"H2617","sense":"Chesed — steadfast, loyal covenant love; kindness that keeps its promises.","anchor":"Zechariah 7:9","en":"kindness","text":"“Thus has Yahweh of Armies spoken, saying, ‘Execute true judgment, and show kindness and compassion every man to his brother."},{"strongs":"H7356","sense":"Compassion — tender mercy; related to the word for womb.","anchor":"Zechariah 7:9","en":"compassion","text":"“Thus has Yahweh of Armies spoken, saying, ‘Execute true judgment, and show kindness and compassion every man to his brother."},{"strongs":"H2580","sense":"Grace, favour — kindness shown to the undeserving.","anchor":"Zechariah 12:10","en":"grace","text":"I will pour on David’s house, and on the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace and of supplication; and they will look to me whom they have pierced; and they shall mourn for him, as one mourns for his only son, and will grieve bitterly for him, as one grieves for his firstborn."}]},{"id":"power-hebrew","name":"Strength & Might","lang":"Hebrew","english":["might","power","mighty","strong","strength"],"intro":"Zechariah 4:6 sets two Hebrew strength-words against the Spirit: ‘Not by might, nor by power’.","members":[{"strongs":"H2428","sense":"Might — force, army, wealth; the strength of resources and numbers.","anchor":"Zechariah 4:6","en":"might","text":"Then he answered and spoke to me, saying, “This is Yahweh’s word to Zerubbabel, saying, ‘Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit,’ says Yahweh of Armies."},{"strongs":"H3581","sense":"Power — personal strength, the ability to do.","anchor":"Zechariah 4:6","en":"power","text":"Then he answered and spoke to me, saying, “This is Yahweh’s word to Zerubbabel, saying, ‘Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit,’ says Yahweh of Armies."},{"strongs":"H1368","sense":"Mighty one — a warrior, a hero; God as champion.","anchor":"Zephaniah 3:17","en":"mighty","text":"Yahweh, your God, is among you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you in his love. He will rejoice over you with singing."},{"strongs":"H2388","sense":"To be strong — to take hold, be firm, be courageous.","anchor":"Haggai 2:4","en":"strong","text":"Yet now be strong, Zerubbabel,’ says Yahweh. ‘Be strong, Joshua, son of Jehozadak, the high priest. Be strong, all you people of the land,’ says Yahweh, ‘and work, for I am with you,’ says Yahweh of Armies."},{"strongs":"H5797","sense":"Strength — might, security, stronghold.","anchor":"Psalm 46:1","en":"strength","text":""}]},{"id":"time-hebrew","name":"Time, Day & Forever","lang":"Hebrew","english":["time","day","feasts","forever"],"intro":"Hebrew distinguishes the season for something, the day, the appointed time and the long age.","members":[{"strongs":"H6256","sense":"Time, season — the right time for something.","anchor":"Haggai 1:2","en":"time","text":"“This is what Yahweh of Armies says: These people say, ‘The time hasn’t yet come, the time for Yahweh’s house to be built.’”"},{"strongs":"H3117","sense":"Day — a literal day, and ‘the day of Yahweh’.","anchor":"Zephaniah 1:14","en":"day","text":"The great day of Yahweh is near. It is near, and hurries greatly, the voice of the day of Yahweh. The mighty man cries there bitterly."},{"strongs":"H4150","sense":"Appointed time — a fixed meeting or feast.","anchor":"Zechariah 8:19","en":"feasts","text":"Yahweh of Armies says: “The fasts of the fourth, fifth, seventh, and tenth months shall be for the house of Judah joy and gladness, and cheerful feasts. Therefore love truth and peace.”"},{"strongs":"H5769","sense":"Olam — age, long duration, forever.","anchor":"Malachi 1:4","en":"forever","text":"Whereas Edom says, “We are beaten down, but we will return and build the waste places”; Yahweh of Armies says, “They shall build, but I will throw down; and men will call them ‘The Wicked Land,’ even the people against whom Yahweh shows wrath forever.”"}]}];
